Two blocks of no bytes are equal without memcmp being given a null pointer

MemoryBlock::matches(), which is how two blocks are compared, calls memcmp()
whenever the two sizes are equal -- including when both are zero. A block of no
bytes holds no address, so memcmp() is given a null pointer, which its
declaration forbids however many bytes it is asked to compare:

  modules/juce_core/memory/juce_MemoryBlock.cpp:122:24: runtime error: null
  pointer passed as argument 1, which is declared to never be null

Comparing an empty block with an empty block is ordinary: a plug-in whose
getStateInformation() has nothing to say leaves the block it is given empty,
and comparing two such states is how a host, or a test, sees that nothing
changed. Nothing is read either way, so the answer stays as it was; only the
call goes away.

A fuzz target of ADLplug-Next found it, one that reads and writes the plug-in's
state and compares what comes out.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by
cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is
upstream.

diff --git a/modules/juce_core/memory/juce_MemoryBlock.cpp b/modules/juce_core/memory/juce_MemoryBlock.cpp
index 6ae8a4fec2..8f13af2999 100644
--- a/modules/juce_core/memory/juce_MemoryBlock.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_core/memory/juce_MemoryBlock.cpp
@@ -118,8 +118,10 @@ bool MemoryBlock::operator!= (const MemoryBlock& other) const noexcept
 
 bool MemoryBlock::matches (const void* dataToCompare, size_t dataSize) const noexcept
 {
+    // A block of no bytes has no address to hand memcmp, which may not be given
+    // a null pointer even when there is nothing at all to compare.
     return size == dataSize
-            && memcmp (data, dataToCompare, size) == 0;
+            && (size == 0 || memcmp (data, dataToCompare, size) == 0);
 }
 
